  lesson:
    'The first scholar in this part of the study is Dr. Cheikh Anta Diop, and the place to begin is the world he was born into. He was born on December 29, 1923, at Diourbel in Senegal, which was then a colony in French West Africa. The schools, the courts, the textbooks and the museums answered to Paris. And the lesson that system taught, sometimes in so many words and more often by silence, was that Africa had no history of its own: that the continent had waited, without writing or states or science, until Europe arrived. Ancient Egypt was the one great problem for that lesson, because no one could deny the pyramids, the calendar, the medicine or the monumental writing. So Egypt was quietly moved. It was taught as a civilization of the Mediterranean or of Asia that happened to stand on African soil, its builders an imported people. Diop described the result years later in the UNESCO General History of Africa. In most textbooks, he wrote, "it is simply and flatly asserted that the Egyptians were white and the honest layman is left with the impression that any such assertion must necessarily have a prior basis of solid research." Then he answered it: "But there is no such basis, as this chapter has shown. And so generation after generation has been misled." That sentence is the reason he belongs among the voices that were not heard. In 1946, at twenty-three, Diop went to Paris to study. He took his first degree in philosophy and then studied chemistry, and later nuclear physics in the laboratory of Frédéric Joliot-Curie at the Collège de France. He was not only in the library. In his own account of his life’s work, the preface he called The Meaning of Our Work, he says he began his research in September 1946 and served from 1950 to 1953 as Secretary General of the students of the RDA, the African Democratic Rally, the movement pressing for independence in French Africa. For him, recovering Africa’s history and winning Africa’s freedom were one fight, and he saw the colonial classroom as part of what had to be undone. In 1951 he registered a thesis subject at the University of Paris on who the pre-dynastic Egyptians were. The research became his first book, Nations nègres et culture, published in 1954. The doctorate he received in 1960 was on a different subject, a comparison of the political and social systems of Europe and Africa, which he published as L’Afrique noire précoloniale, in English Precolonial Black Africa. A story is often told that a Paris jury refused his Egypt thesis on political grounds; we could not verify that story from a source we could read, so we do not tell it here, and the record we can read is strong enough without it. From 1961 he was on the staff of IFAN, the Fundamental Institute of Black Africa in Dakar, where, in his translator’s words, he directed the radiocarbon laboratory which he founded. Think about what that means. The man who argued about the age and origin of civilizations built the instrument that measures age. In 1966, at the First World Festival of Negro Arts, he shared a special award with W. E. B. DuBois as the writer who had exerted the greatest influence on Black thought in the twentieth century. After his death in 1986, the University of Dakar was renamed Cheikh Anta Diop University. Now his message, in overview, because the lessons that follow take it piece by piece. First, he held that ancient Egypt was an African civilization, born in the Nile Valley among African peoples. In a 1956 address in Paris, quoted by his translator, he said, "we have come to discover that the ancient Pharaonic Egyptian civilization was undoubtedly a Negro civilization." The word Negro was the ordinary scholarly word of his time and language; he meant Black African. Second, he held that this was not a matter of pride but of method: African history cannot be written correctly while its oldest and best-documented chapter is cut away. He compared it to a European historian trying to write Europe’s history without Greece and Rome. Third, he refused to win the argument with myth. This is the line we should remember him by. He wrote that it could seem too tempting "to delude the masses engaged in a struggle for national independence by taking liberties with scientific truth, by unveiling a mythical, embellished past." He would not do it. He brought physical anthropology, the reports of Greek travelers, Egyptian art, language comparison and a laboratory test of skin samples from mummies, and he asked his readers to check him. Fourth, he called for workers, not fans. He wanted teams of honest, bold researchers, and in 1971 he asked UNESCO for an international meeting in Egypt where Egyptologists and Africanists would compare their evidence face to face for the first time. UNESCO accepted. That meeting was held in Cairo from 28 January to 3 February 1974, with twenty scholars present, and its report is printed in UNESCO’s own General History of Africa. The report does not say Diop won every point. It records that most of the experts did not accept his claim that the Nile Valley population was homogeneous from the earliest times, and that on the colours in Egyptian art nothing was conceded by either side. But it also records this, in the rapporteur’s own words: not all participants had prepared communications comparable with "the painstakingly researched contributions of Professors Cheikh Anta Diop and Obenga." And it adds, "There was consequently a real lack of balance in the discussions." That is the record telling on itself, and it is the kind of evidence this study values most. Today the mainstream states plainly that Egyptian civilization was an indigenous development of the Nile Valley, an African civilization; that was the heart of what he was mocked for saying. Some of his particular claims remain contested, and we name them narrowly in each lesson. Now we weigh him by the Word, and we do it honestly in both directions. Where the record was rewritten, the Word is not surprised. Daniel saw a power that would {{kjv:Daniel 7:25|think to change times and laws}}, and our Lord promised the opposite end: {{kjv:Luke 8:17}} Where Diop insisted that Egypt belongs to Africa’s family, the Word said it first, in the table of nations: {{kjv:Genesis 10:6}} Mizraim is the Hebrew name for Egypt, and the Psalms call Egypt {{kjv:Psalm 105:23|the land of Ham}}. The Word even tells on Egypt’s greatness itself: Moses {{kjv:Acts 7:22|was learned in all the wisdom of the Egyptians}}, and Solomon’s wisdom is praised as greater than {{kjv:1 Kings 4:30|all the wisdom of Egypt}}. Scripture never pretended Egypt was small. But Diop also went beyond the record into claims the Word corrects. In the same preface he claimed that Judaism and Islam are rooted in Egyptian cosmogony, that the Egyptian god osiris is a figure "essentially identifiable with Christ," and that "certain Biblical passages are practically copies of Egyptian moral texts." Those are his claims, and they are wrong. The Scriptures did not come from Egypt’s temples: {{kjv:2 Peter 1:21}} The worlds were not explained by Egypt’s cosmogony; they were framed by the Word of Yahweh: {{kjv:Hebrews 11:3}} And Jesus is not a copy of a river god; He is the Lamb of Yahweh, the Eternal Son, who died and rose in history before witnesses. His first lesson takes those claims one by one. What does Diop mean for us now? He shows us a scholar who loved his people enough to refuse to lie to them, and that is a rare kind of love. He teaches us to ask of every textbook, every documentary and every preacher, what is the evidence? And he reminds us that a record rewritten by power does not stay rewritten, because Yahweh is the One we ask, {{kjv:Psalm 43:3|O send out thy light and thy truth}}. We honour him for the evidence he gathered and the courage it took, and we weigh every word he said, as we weigh every word anyone says, by the Word that does not change.',
