      body: 'The youth Bible study at New Covenant Baptist in Atlanta had a new rule, and it came from a book none of them had expected to read. Sister Renee Daniels, a retired librarian, had brought a copy of The African Origin of Civilization to class and read them one sentence from the preface, where Diop says what he wants is teams, not of passive readers, but of honest, bold research workers. Then she closed the book and said, “That is us. We are going to be a team.” So for six weeks the teenagers became researchers. Kayla took the Greek writers and found out what Herodotus actually said, and also what he said did not prove anything. Marcus took maps and found that his middle school textbook had never once shown Egypt as part of Africa. Tre, who liked arguing, took Diop’s chapter of arguments against him and reported on it with the honesty of a referee. And Imani, the quiet one, took the Bible. She came back the third week with Genesis 10 open on her phone. “Mizraim is Egypt,” she said. “And it is right next to Cush. The Bible put Egypt in Africa’s family before anybody wrote a textbook.” The class went quiet and then loud. In the fifth week they hit a hard part. Diop’s preface says Bible passages are copies of Egyptian texts. Tre said, “So is he right?” Sister Daniels did not answer. She handed them Romans 2 and 2 Peter 1 and said, “Research it.” Imani found the verse about the law written in the hearts of every nation, and Kayla found that holy men spoke as the Holy Ghost moved them. By the end they wrote their own one-page report: Egypt is African, the textbook was wrong, Diop was right about that; Diop was wrong about the Bible, and the Word shows why. They presented it on Youth Sunday. The pastor asked them to keep the team going. They named it after the line that started it all, but they changed one word. They called it Not Passive Readers of the Word.',
