      body: 'The Afrocentric reading circle that met at a community center on the South Side of Chicago had been going for eleven years, and Brother Jamal Price was its most passionate member. He had read every Diop book in English, and he had a favorite idea: that Africa’s ancient southern cradle had been naturally gentle, just and peaceful, and that the cruelty of history had all come from the north. One night a newer member, a young nurse named Simone Carter, asked a quiet question. “If our cradle was so gentle, how do you explain the slave markets on our own coasts? Some of the sellers were African kings.” The room got tense. Jamal started to answer and then stopped. He had no answer that did not sound like an excuse. Simone was a member of a small Pentecostal church, and she had been praying about whether to keep coming to the circle. She spoke carefully. “I love what Dr. Diop found. The mother-line kingdoms, the record of our history, all of it. But I cannot believe any people is innocent by nature. My Bible says every one of us has sinned, every people, north and south.” Nobody spoke for a while. Then an older man, Mr. Ellis, who had marched in Selma, said, “She is right. I have seen cruelty in every color. I have seen grace in every color too.” Jamal went home that night and did not sleep well. A week later he called Simone and asked if he could visit her church. He sat in the back for three Sundays. On the fourth, he heard a sermon on Romans 3 and stayed afterward to talk to the pastor. He told the circle later that he still loved Diop’s history. “But I was trying to find an innocent people,” he said, “and there is not one. There is only an innocent Lamb.” The circle kept meeting. Now they open every session by reading one passage of Scripture before they open any other book.',
